dn't have to like run everything it'll  be a little bit smart about like only  run the parts that need to be run  because those are the only scripts that  you've changed  um i also  in general i tend towards leaning  towards using uh an application called  make  and essentially it's doing the same  thing except it's not specific to r  and there was a talk about make from  that talk that i have linked  that you can look up  later so next the other thing is that  dissertation has a bunch of different  components to it so what do you do about  all of those other related projects  in this overall  project so  for example in my work i had at least  eight totally separate repositories and  i needed a way to keep track of all of  them  and so yes you can have a heuristic  where you're like okay every time you  clone down the repository make sure it's  all in the same folder  that works um but it still means that  like i have to like go make sure i hunt  down like all seven or eight of these  links and then make sure that i clone  them in all in the same place etc etc  so like i had a whole series of projects  around that were booked down based so  this was before quarto came out and was  a thing  um i had you know certain projects that  were mixed and weren't using uh  reproducible format so like my irb  application was this word doc that i had  to submit some of my presentations were  on google slides  so i just downloaded those  into a repository as a pdf just so i had  them but they weren't really in a  reproducible format  and then the other issue that i had was  um  finding a way to collaborate with and  without git so mainly with my actual  dissertation writing um  i ended up pushing up to my git repo and  then i had my advisor  look at the repository since it was  synced up with overleaf and so like all  of her comments were through overleaf  and i had to do the work of like  synchronizing all of that work down and  i don't really know of a great way to  like other than using overleaf  where you can like coordinate on writing  like a technical document  where one of those party members doesn't  know how to use like git as a version  control system but this is sort of like  my work around  around all of that  as you are  writing these like really complicated  documents one of the things i i would  highly suggest you figure out at the  very beginning is learn how to use the  child documents and whatever system  you're using and so for me my whole  dissertation was written in latex and so  like i had this main text file  and using  sub files you can create a link to  another file  and then in that link so this was in the  introduction chapter of my dissertation  and then so in the introduction chapter  it had a whole bunch of other  subsections to it and each of those  subsections was linked to another file  and the reason for this was again i had  this weird like overleaf integration  thing going on and so if anything  horribly happened it only happened in  like  the three paragraphs of writing that was  going on and i also didn't have to  scroll down through like thousands and  thousands of lines when i just needed to  edit a certain thing so child documents  does end up making the actual project  repository look more complex but it's  just so much more  like usable and like  yeah i'll leave it at that um it's a lot  more usable  um just because you're not staring at  like hundreds and thousands of lines of  like just straight up text  and then so for something as complicated  as like compiling a latex document this  is where i use a make file  and one of the things that i put into my  make file is i by default make defines a  of command called all  and then what i'd write in my make files  is i put in two hashes the name of the  command and then like some simple  description  and then  with the power of grep and said  what this allows me to do when i set up  my makefile is when i run the command  make it sort of just dumps out like  here's a little shortcut of all the  things you can run and this helps you  from you know catting this file and  trying to figure out what are all the  things that i can do  if you leave like these little  breadcrumbs for yourself  you can get like a nice little output  like this and this is this helps a lot  when you're like you know sort of  panicked writing um and trying to just  get things working um towards the end  and so what i would actually change is  since quarto is now a thing and it  allows you to sort of use  latex templates that would be the next  thing that i would try to look into  i had thought about using  knitter and latex like the rnw files but  i still have to synchronize everything  with overleaf because my just so i can  get feedback from my advisor and so like  i don't know if that's still like a  great idea mainly because like i still  had to do that overleaf integration  but if i didn't that's what i would  actually do because then i would  actually be able to load up those  resources in r into like the actual tech  document  so i talked about all the different uh  repositories that i had and so that was  the other big challenge of like how do  you actually go and keep all of these  pieces together  and so  the way that i ended up using this  was through  using some part of git called git sub  modules  and so one of the rules around using git  is don't nest your git repositories like  if you click clone or init something  nowhere in any of those subfolders  should you get clone or init another  folder  but git sub module sort of allows you to  have like sort of have that feature  available to you  and so what i ended up doing was i had  this big giant project called my  dissertation and then in a subfolder i  had all of my other git repositories  and then so for example my analysis  uh repository which was its own totally  separate git repo  i had it  and  i was able to access it as if it was  just a regular subfolder and it was  treated as two totally separate git  repositories and so that's what git sub  modules allow you to do  i'm not going to go over how to use git  sub modules but essentially each sub  module is its own repository that you  probably if you've no git and github  just treat it like a regular repository  the only difference is when you commit  and push to github you have to go back  to the parent repository and then do  another round of adding and committing  and that second round of adding  committing is all it's doing is keeping  track of like the actual commits  that it should reference to in the sub  module  but it  the other way you can think about it is  when you use git sub modules the mental  model kind of is like nested git  repositories and you just have to track  the one inside totally separate from the  one outside but you have to do it twice  and so if you look at the actual parent  repository it will look like this on  github and you'll see that it's really  only tracking like the last get state  like that's why you have to make this  separate commit on the parent repository  but inside is totally normal  and you can just treat it as if you  weren't working in a git repository and  that's really nice but now the benefit  is i can clone down one repository and  all eight repositories can come down  with it and i can rely on that um  relationship between those subfolders if  i needed to  and then the last thing was the actual  like experiments some of the experiments  that i ran  um so in short i would really like so i  ran an experiment that relied on uh  shiny  but i sort of like  out of time like sort of just did  something really janky with it  but if i were to do that part over there  is this library called shiny surveys  where you can actually use shiny to  administer and give out surveys and so  and what that also allows you to do is  you can build in like a randomization  process in there so like people can come  to the app put in an id number and then  they get randomized to something and  then you can administer your survey that  way and i would have done i should have  done things that way except for me i  just ended up like as people were coming  into the workshop to take the survey i  would like note i would note their names  and then i would do the randomization  for them and then give them back their  ids and it was like this whole like mess  that took like an insane amount of time  in the beginning so that's something  that i definitely would have uh  worked on better  so in sum what i would have changed is i  would make a description file and put in  that type equals project use rig to  manage multiple versions of r since  other things i had other projects going  on  targets and a make file i should  probably have used those a little bit  more but i didn't  quarto is now a really cool thing like  this presentation's written in quarto  and i really want to be able to  explore that more and then shiny surveys  so  with that thanks  [Applause]  you
 

